[2026-03-16T03:01:09.521Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RLiLUJhdQPpFou9fEyuC36","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J7oAtRQxM2cy5WUtyt5yHC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1527,"cache_read_input_tokens":15651,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1527,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LeLFvavCQVL4MjNXQBs2ss","session_id":"6a740ffb-b2f5-4751-aabf-5f2a1bcc96e7","uuid":"01a3f181-00d8-4233-95d8-3a293f0b01f9"}
[2026-03-16T03:01:09.569Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7oAtRQxM2cy5WUtyt5yHC","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You are writing the database and code for the app to match the specs in AppSpec.md and docs/tests.md, and to match an optional style guide. If `AppRevisions.md` exists, it describes new functionality and spec changes organized by topic section, and must also be followed.\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→Unpack the initial write app task into subtasks using `add-task`:\n     8→\n     9→First, add a setup task. The SetupApp subtask must complete ALL scaffolding, dependency\n    10→installation, configuration (vite, tsconfig, netlify.toml, playwright config), and run\n    11→`npm run check` successfully before any component writing begins. Do not leave infrastructure\n    12→setup for component-writing tasks to discover and fix:\n    13→\n    14→```bash\n    15→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    16→[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"SetupApp: Setup the app\", \"DesignDatabase: Design the database\"] }]\n    17→EOF\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→Then add ALL page tasks in a single `add-task` call. Write out every page and component\n    21→explicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n    22→```bash\n    23→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    24→[\n    25→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n    26→    \"WriteComponent<Component1>: Write the <Component1> component\",\n    27→    \"WriteComponent<Component2>: Write the <Component2> component\",\n    28→    \"WritePage<Page1>: Write the page itself\"\n    29→  ]},\n    30→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n    31→    \"WriteComponent<Component3>: Write the <Component3> component\",\n    32→    \"WritePage<Page2>: Write the page itself\"\n    33→  ]}\n    34→]\n    35→EOF\n    36→```\n    37→\n    38→## SetupApp Guidance\n    39→\n    40→During the SetupApp subtask, the app directory is mostly empty. Do not waste time searching\n    41→for files that don't exist yet — create them. In particular:\n    42→\n    43→- **`store.ts`**: The Redux store file does not exist yet during setup. Create it at\n    44→  `src/store.ts` rather than searching for it.\n    45→- **Install Playwright and Replay as dev dependencies** during setup so they are available\n    46→  when test-writing begins. Run `npm install --save-dev @playwright/test @replayio/playwright`\n    47→  and `npx replayio install` during SetupApp, not later when tests are first run.\n    48→- **Standard directory structure**: Create these directories up front:\n    49→  - `src/pages/` — page components\n    50→  - `src/components/` — shared UI components\n    51→  - `src/slices/` — Redux slices\n    52→  - `netlify/functions/` — backend serverless functions\n    53→  - `scripts/` — build/db scripts (schema.ts, seed-db.ts, check.ts, test.ts)\n    54→  - `tests/` — Playwright test files\n    55→\n    56→## Database Provisioning\n    57→\n    58→For Neon database setup during app creation, follow `skills/scripts/neon-setup.md`. Key points:\n    59→\n    60→- `NEON_API_KEY` is available as a container-level environment variable.\n    61→- Use `$NEON_API_KEY` directly in curl headers (shell expansion). Do not use `printenv` in a subshell.\n    62→- **If authentication fails**, the variable may contain a trailing newline. Capture it via\n    63→  `printf` first: `NEON_KEY=$(printf '%s' \"$NEON_API_KEY\")` then use `$NEON_KEY` in curl headers.\n    64→- After creating a project, save the `NEON_PROJECT_ID` and `DATABASE_URL` to `.env`.\n    65→- See `skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for the full list of required environment variables.\n    66→\n    67→## Required Configuration Files\n    68→\n    69→The SetupApp subtask must create all of the following configuration files. Do NOT explore\n    70→git history to discover these — create them directly using the patterns below:\n    71→\n    72→- **`package.json`** — Dependencies, scripts (`check`, `test`, `deploy`), type: module\n    73→- **`tsconfig.json`** — Strict mode, JSX react-jsx, ES2020 target, path aliases\n    74→- **`vite.config.ts`** — React plugin, sourcemap: true, minify: false, process.env.NODE_ENV define\n    75→- **`netlify.toml`** — `base` set to app subdirectory, redirects for `/api/*` and SPA catch-all\n    76→- **`playwright.config.ts`** — Replay browser, reporter, timeouts, webServer with `--functions`\n    77→- **`index.html`** — Vite entry point\n    78→- **`src/main.tsx`** — React root with Provider and Router\n    79→- **`src/App.tsx`** — Router outlet / layout\n    80→- **`src/store.ts`** — Redux store\n    81→- **`src/index.css`** — Global styles and CSS variables\n    82→- **`scripts/check.ts`** — Typecheck + lint quality gate\n    83→- **`scripts/test.ts`** — Test runner with Neon branch management\n    84→- **`scripts/schema.ts`** — `initSchema` function (single source of truth for DB schema)\n    85→- **`scripts/seed-db.ts`** — Seed data with `truncateAndSeed`\n    86→- **`scripts/deploy.ts`** — Production deployment script\n    87→- **`.eslintrc.cjs`** or **`eslint.config.*`** — ESLint configuration\n    88→- **`netlify/functions/db.ts`** — Shared `getSql()` database helper\n    89→\n    90→## Reference Apps\n    91→\n    92→When scaffolding a new app, check for existing reference apps that can inform your setup.\n    93→Look for completed apps under `apps/` first — if any exist, read their configuration files\n    94→directly (e.g., `vite.config.ts`, `tsconfig.json`, `netlify.toml`, `playwright.config.ts`).\n    95→If no apps exist locally, use `git log --all --oneline` to find previous app builds in git\n    96→history, then use `git show <commit>:<path>` to read their configuration files. Prefer\n    97→reading existing local apps over git history exploration to avoid excessive shell commands.\n    98→\n    99→## Guidelines\n   100→\n   101→- Write clean, working code. No TODOs, placeholder implementations, or mock data. All features must be real and fully functional end-to-end, backed by the database.\n   102→- All JSX rendered on a page must be abstracted into other React components with their own files.\n   103→- Check for style guides at both `apps/AppStyle.md` (shared across all apps) and `apps/<AppName>/AppStyle.md` (app-specific). Read both if they exist and use them to style the pages and components appropriately. The app-specific style guide takes precedence when they conflict. Prefer using CSS files with style variables instead of hardcoded styles.\n   104→- Check for reusable library and component code in `apps/shared` and use these when appropriate. Set up the app build system so it can use code from this directory.\n   105→\n   106→## Database Schema\n   107→\n   108→The app MUST have a reusable `initSchema` function exported from a shared module (e.g.,\n   109→`scripts/schema.ts`). This is the single source of truth for the database schema, used by\n   110→the test and deploy scripts (see `skills/scripts/test.md` and `skills/scripts/deploy.md`).\n   111→\n   112→**Requirements for `initSchema`**:\n   113→\n   114→- Exported function that accepts a database URL string and returns a Promise. It must NOT read\n   115→  `DATABASE_URL` from the environment or `.env` — the caller passes the URL.\n   116→- Must use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` for all tables, making it idempotent and safe to re-run.\n   117→- Must create all tables, indices, and constraints needed by the app.\n   118→- When a new table or column is added to the app, it MUST be added to `initSchema`. There must be\n   119→  exactly one place where the schema is defined.\n   120→\n   121→The app should also have migration logic for `ALTER TABLE` changes (new columns, new indices)\n   122→that `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` cannot detect. Migrations run after `initSchema` in all\n   123→contexts (testing, deployment).\n   124→\n   125→## Directives\n   126→\n   127→- Always run `npm run check` from the **app directory** (`cd /repo/apps/AppName && npm run check`),\n   128→  not standalone `npx tsc --noEmit` at the repo root. The app directory has the correct `tsconfig.json`\n   129→  and ESLint config. See `skills/scripts/check.md`.\n   130→\n   131→- When working on a component, identify the test entries in the spec relevant to that component, and make sure that the entry requirements are satisfied by the app code.\n   132→\n   133→- Match the visual appearance of elements to the mockup: button variants, icon presence, badge/tag styling, section layouts (horizontal vs vertical), and field sets. Do not simplify the visual design.\n   134→\n   135→- All components must include `data-testid` attributes on interactive elements, containers, list items,\n   136→  and anything that E2E tests will need to target. Add testids during initial component development,\n   137→  not retroactively. Reference docs/tests.md to identify which elements need testids.\n   138→\n   139→- In a monorepo where the app is in a subdirectory (e.g., `apps/SalesCRM`), add `base = \"apps/SalesCRM\"`\n   140→  to `netlify.toml` so that Netlify CLI resolves the functions directory relative to the app root,\n   141→  not the git repository root. IMPORTANT: even with `base` set, `netlify dev` may still resolve the\n   142→  functions directory incorrectly. The Playwright config's `webServer` must pass `--functions ./netlify/functions`\n   143→  explicitly. When curling endpoints manually, start the server the same way:\n   144→  `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`.\n   145→\n   146→- All frontend code must use the `/api/` prefix for calling Netlify Functions (e.g.,\n   147→  `fetch('/api/my-function')`). Do NOT use `/.netlify/functions/` — this path returns 404\n   148→  with Netlify Functions v2. The `/api/` prefix is the standard for all function calls in\n   149→  both development and production.\n   150→\n   151→- **Ensure `netlify.toml` or `_redirects` correctly routes `/api/*` to Netlify Functions.**\n   152→  Without explicit routing rules, the SPA catch-all redirect (`/* /index.html 200`) will\n   153→  serve HTML for API endpoint requests instead of routing them to functions. This is the most\n   154→  common cause of `curl` returning HTML instead of JSON when testing deployed API endpoints.\n   155→  Place API redirects before the SPA catch-all:\n   156→  ```toml\n   157→  [[redirects]]\n   158→    from = \"/api/*\"\n   159→    to = \"/.netlify/functions/:splat\"\n   160→    status = 200\n   161→\n   162→  [[redirects]]\n   163→    from = \"/*\"\n   164→    to = \"/index.html\"\n   165→    status = 200\n   166→  ```\n   167→\n   168→- Netlify functions must not import `@neondatabase/serverless` directly. Instead, each app must\n   169→  have a shared `netlify/functions/db.ts` module that all functions import:\n   170→\n   171→  ```typescript\n   172→  import { neon } from '@neondatabase/serverless'\n   173→\n   174→  export function getSql() {\n   175→    return neon(process.env.DATABASE_URL!)\n   176→  }\n   177→  ```\n   178→\n   179→  - `DATABASE_URL` points to the production Neon database in deployment, and to an ephemeral\n   180→    Neon branch during testing. The test script manages branch creation and cleanup.\n   181→\n   182→- When using the sql function from `db.ts`, ONLY use tagged template literal\n   183→  syntax for queries: `` sql`SELECT * FROM table WHERE id = ${id}` ``. NEVER use `sql(queryString, paramsArray)`.\n   184→  For dynamic WHERE clauses, build composable query fragments and conditionally include them in the tagged template.\n   185→\n   186→- **Handle `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` with Neon query results.** When `tsconfig.json` enables\n   187→  `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` (included in strict mode), accessing array elements by index (e.g.,\n   188→  `rows[0]`) returns `T | undefined`. For Neon query results, always check for undefined before\n   189→  accessing properties: `const row = rows[0]; if (!row) return notFound();`. Do NOT use non-null\n   190→  assertions (`rows[0]!`) to silence the error — handle the undefined case properly. This is the\n   191→  most common TypeScript error during app development with Neon, requiring multiple `npm run check`\n   192→  iterations to resolve.\n   193→\n   194→- For database columns with DATE, TIMESTAMP, or UUID types, always convert empty strings to null\n   195→  before inserting or updating. Use `value || null` instead of `value ?? null`, because the nullish\n   196→  coalescing operator (`??`) does not convert empty strings.\n   197→\n   198→- When formatting date values received from the API/database for display or for use as\n   199→  `<input type=\"date\">` values, always normalize the string to `YYYY-MM-DD` first (e.g.,\n   200→  `value.split('T')[0]`). PostgreSQL serializes DATE columns as full ISO timestamps (e.g.,\n   201→  `1985-03-15T00:00:00.000Z`), not plain `YYYY-MM-DD` strings. HTML date inputs reject any\n   202→  format other than `YYYY-MM-DD`, rendering the field empty. Likewise, passing an ISO timestamp\n   203→  to `new Date()` with an appended timezone anchor produces an invalid date.\n   204→\n   205→- When a backend handler deletes and re-inserts related records (e.g., delete-then-reinsert\n   206→  pattern for updating child rows), it must first delete any rows in other tables that have\n   207→  foreign key references to the rows being deleted. Walk the FK dependency chain from leaf\n   208→  tables inward before deleting the target rows.\n   209→\n   210→- Netlify Functions accessed at `/.netlify/functions/<name>/<resourceId>` have the function name at\n   211→  path index 2 and the resource ID at index 3 (after splitting on `/` and filtering empty segments).\n   212→  Common off-by-one error: using index 2 for the resource ID when it contains the function name.\n   213→\n   214→- Detail/record pages should use inline editing (click-to-edit fields, inline selectors, inline\n   215→  dropdowns, etc.) for editing properties of the record rather than opening an edit modal. Inline\n   216→  editing keeps the user in context and is simpler to implement and test. Only use modals for\n   217→  complex multi-step workflows or confirmations (e.g. delete confirmation).\n   218→\n   219→- When building modals that reference other entities (e.g., adding a relationship to a person), use a\n   220→  searchable select/dropdown component, not a plain text input for IDs.\n   221→\n   222→- Redux async thunk `.fulfilled` handlers for mutation operations (create, update, delete) must\n   223→  update the relevant `state.items` array with the response data, not just close modals or clear\n   224→  editing state. Relying solely on a background refetch (`dispatch(fetchAll())`) after a mutation\n   225→  causes stale data if the user interacts with the UI before the refetch completes. Always apply\n   226→  the mutation result to local state immediately in the fulfilled handler.\n   227→\n   228→- When a `useEffect` derives local state from a URL parameter or route param (e.g., setting a\n   229→  `viewingItemId` from a URL's `:id` segment), it must handle both the truthy case (param present →\n   230→  set state) and the falsy case (param absent → clear state). Omitting the `else` branch causes\n   231→  stale state to persist when navigating away from a parameterized route back to the base route.\n   232→\n   233→- When implementing bidirectional sync between URL params and Redux state using separate\n   234→  `useEffect` hooks (one for URL→state, another for state→URL), the state-to-URL effect must\n   235→  not redirect on the initial render. On mount, Redux state is still at its initial value before\n   236→  the URL→state effect has run, so the state-to-URL effect sees \"no selection\" and redirects away\n   237→  from a valid URL. Use a ref to skip the first render or guard against redirecting when state has\n   238→  never been set from the URL.\n   239→\n   240→- Gate dependent API calls on prerequisite data being loaded. When a component fetches data\n   241→  that depends on another piece of state (e.g., fetching dashboard stats requires `currentUser`\n   242→  to be loaded first), add a guard condition to the `useEffect` or thunk that checks for the\n   243→  prerequisite before dispatching. Without this, race conditions cause fetches to fire before\n   244→  prerequisite data is available, resulting in empty or wrong responses that break tests.\n   245→\n   246→- When writing SQL queries for lookup/autocomplete endpoints that return distinct entities, use\n   247→  `DISTINCT ON (<primary_identifier>)` (PostgreSQL) rather than `SELECT DISTINCT` across multiple\n   248→  columns. `SELECT DISTINCT col1, col2` deduplicates on the combination of all listed columns, so\n   249→  rows with the same primary identifier but different secondary values (e.g., same code with\n   250→  different descriptions) will appear as separate results, causing duplicate entries in dropdowns.\n   251→\n   252→- Multi-page apps must include a navigation sidebar or menu component that provides links to all\n   253→  top-level pages. The navigation must be present on every page via the app's root layout. Do not\n   254→  rely on users manually entering URLs to navigate between pages.\n   255→\n   256→- Navigation sidebars and menus must not contain duplicate links pointing to the same URL. Each\n   257→  navigation item must have a unique route. Remove or consolidate any entries that would navigate\n   258→  to the same destination.\n   259→\n   260→- SQL queries in backend functions must only reference tables and columns that are defined in the\n   261→  app's schema (`scripts/schema.ts`). Before writing a query that references a table, verify it\n   262→  exists in `initSchema`. Referencing non-existent tables causes runtime \"relation does not exist\"\n   263→  errors that silently break entire endpoints.\n   264→\n   265→- Do not use `type=\"number\"` on input fields that display formatted values requiring trailing zeros\n   266→  (e.g., currency amounts like \"$175.00\"). HTML number inputs normalize their value, stripping\n   267→  trailing zeros. Use `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` and format the displayed value\n   268→  explicitly (e.g., `toFixed(2)` for currency).\n   269→\n   270→- Navigation sidebars must be collapsible. Include a toggle button that switches between expanded\n   271→  (full labels) and collapsed (icons only) states. When collapsed, show icon-only items at a narrow\n   272→  width with title tooltips for accessibility.\n   273→\n   274→- Never use native HTML `<select>` elements for filter controls or dropdowns when the app has a\n   275→  custom design system or style guide. Native form elements cannot be fully styled and will revert\n   276→  to browser defaults on interaction. Always use custom dropdown components with React state.\n   277→\n   278→- Attachment displays should show file-type-specific icons or thumbnails (e.g., image preview for\n   279→  images, document icon for PDFs, spreadsheet icon for CSVs). Do not use a single generic file\n   280→  icon for all attachment types.\n   281→\n   282→- Attachment functionality must support actual file uploads. Any UI that allows adding attachments\n   283→  must include a working file upload mechanism (e.g., file picker, drag-and-drop), not just link entry.\n   284→\n   285→- Every page component must include consistent padding on its root element (`p-6 max-sm:p-3`)\n   286→  so content is never flush against the screen edges. This applies to all pages including\n   287→  centered layouts (auth forms, error pages) — add padding alongside centering utilities.\n   288→\n   289→- Scripts like `scripts/schema.ts` and `scripts/seed-db.ts` must not auto-execute on import.\n   290→  Wrap any top-level execution in a guard so the script only runs when invoked directly, not\n   291→  when imported by the test or deploy scripts:\n   292→  ```ts\n   293→  if (process.argv[1] && import.meta.url.endsWith(process.argv[1])) {\n   294→    initSchema(process.env.DATABASE_URL!).then(() => process.exit(0));\n   295→  }\n   296→  ```\n   297→  Without this guard, importing `initSchema` or `truncateAndSeed` in the test/deploy script\n   298→  triggers unintended side effects (e.g., running against the wrong database).\n   299→\n   300→## Tips\n   301→\n   302→- Production builds must use `sourcemap: true`, `minify: false`, and the React development build in\n   303→  `vite.config.ts` so that Replay recordings show readable source code with full React component names\n   304→  and developer warnings. Configure this with:\n   305→  ```ts\n   306→  build: {\n   307→    sourcemap: true,\n   308→    minify: false,\n   309→  },\n   310→  define: {\n   311→    'process.env.NODE_ENV': '\"development\"',\n   312→  },\n   313→  ```\n   314→  The `define` setting forces React to use its development bundle in production builds, which preserves\n   315→  component display names and enables React DevTools support in Replay recordings. Never remove these settings.\n   316→- When scaffolding a new Vite project, the target directory must be empty. Scaffold in a temp directory\n   317→  (`/tmp/<name>`) and copy the needed files to the app directory.\n   318→- Plan the complete data flow before writing code: what the backend endpoint returns, what the Redux\n   319→  slice state shape looks like, and what props each component needs. Write backend → Redux → sections → modals → page.\n   320→- When significantly modifying an existing file (>50% of lines), use Write to replace the entire file\n   321→  rather than multiple sequential Edits. Avoid chains of 4-5 Edits to the same file.\n   322→- When a React component uses `useState` with a prop as the initial value, the state will NOT update\n   323→  when the prop changes. Add a `useEffect` to sync local state with prop changes, or use the prop\n   324→  directly for display and only use local state during editing.\n   325→- For Redux slices, avoid setting `loading = true` during refetches when data already exists. Only show\n   326→  loading indicators on initial load (`state.items.length === 0`). This prevents \"Loading...\" flashes\n   327→  that cause tests to see empty content during refetches.\n   328→- When an async operation (like saving) should visually complete before closing edit mode, make the\n   329→  `onUpdate`/`onSave` callback return a Promise, and await it before calling `setEditing(false)`.\n   330→- If the backend supports DELETE operations for a resource, the UI must include delete buttons/actions.\n   331→  Check all backend endpoints and ensure the frontend exposes every CRUD operation.\n   332→- When adding domain-specific colors (e.g., status badge colors), always define them as CSS variables\n   333→  in `index.css` under the `@theme` block rather than hardcoding `rgba()`/`rgb()` values in component\n   334→  files. This keeps all colors centralized and easy to theme.\n   335→- Components should be layout-agnostic: do NOT include outer padding (`px-*`) or margin in component\n   336→  root elements. Let the parent page/layout control spacing. When components embed their own padding,\n   337→  composing them in flex rows causes double-padding conflicts that require rework.\n   338→\n   339→- **`jq` is not available in the container.** When you need to parse JSON from shell commands\n   340→  (e.g., Neon API responses, Netlify CLI output), use `python3 -c` or `node -e` instead of\n   341→  `jq`. Example: `curl -s ... | python3 -c \"import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['key'])\"`\n   342→  or `curl -s ... | node -e \"process.stdin.on('data',d=>console.log(JSON.parse(d).key))\"`.\n   343→\n   344→- Prefer using the Glob and Grep tools over shell `find`, `ls`, and `grep` commands for file\n   345→  exploration and content searching. The dedicated tools provide better structured output and\n   346→  avoid unnecessary shell command overhead.\n   347→\n   348→- Run `npm install` once with all required packages rather than running it multiple times with\n   349→  the same or overlapping packages. Batch all dependency additions into a single install command.\n   350→\n   351→- When downloading mockup images for visual reference, use `curl -L -o /tmp/<filename> <url>`.\n   352→  This pattern works well for fetching mockups from UploadThing or other image hosts.\n   353→\n   354→- Do not use `overflow: hidden` on containers that have children with absolutely-positioned elements\n   355→  (e.g., dropdown menus, popovers, tooltips). The overflow clipping will cut off content that extends\n   356→  beyond the container boundary. Additionally, elements that render floating/overlapping UI (such as\n   357→  action menu wrappers) must establish their own stacking context with `position: relative` and an\n   358→  explicit `z-index` so they appear above sibling elements.\n   359→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LeLFvavCQVL4MjNXQBs2ss","session_id":"6a740ffb-b2f5-4751-aabf-5f2a1bcc96e7","uuid":"d25e5a3d-0060-41b5-94ce-2794e7cc38c9"}
